  "output": "Now I have a complete picture. Let me compile the findings.\n\n---\n\n## Performance Analysis\n\n### 1. Missing Database Indexes (highest impact)\n\nThe `conversions` table only has an index on `user_id`. Every admin dashboard query does full table scans on an unindexed table.\n\n**Queries hitting unindexed columns:**\n- `Conversion.where(status: :completed).count` — no index on `status`\n- `Conversion.group(:tool_name)` — no index on `tool_name`\n- Every `WHERE created_at > ?` query — no index on `created_at`\n- `User.where(plan: :pro)` — no index on `plan`\n\n**Fix — generate a migration:**\n```ruby\n# db/migrate/YYYYMMDDHHMMSS_add_performance_indexes.rb\nclass AddPerformanceIndexes < ActiveRecord::Migration[8.1]\n  def change\n    add_index :conversions, :status\n    add_index :conversions, :tool_name\n    add_index :conversions, :created_at\n    add_index :users, :plan\n  end\nend\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. Admin Dashboard — 15 Unbatched Count Queries on Every Hit\n\n`admin/dashboard_controller.rb:7–49` fires **15+ separate SQL COUNT queries** on every page load with zero caching. SolidCache is already installed.\n\n**Fix — cache the whole stats block:**\n```ruby\n# admin/dashboard_controller.rb\ndef index\n  @stats = Rails.cache.fetch(\"admin_dashboard_stats\", expires_in: 5.minutes) do\n    now = Time.current\n    {\n      total_users:           User.count,\n      users_today:           User.where(created_at: now.beginning_of_day..).count,\n      users_this_week:       User.where(created_at: 7.days.ago..).count,\n      # ... rest of the stats hash\n    }\n  end\n  # @recent_users / @recent_conversions stay outside cache (they change every second)\n  @recent_users       = User.order(created_at: :desc).limit(10)\n  @recent_conversions = Conversion.includes(:user).order(created_at: :desc).limit(10)\nend\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. Analytics Controller — 3 Queries Where 1 Works\n\n`analytics_controller.rb:6–11` hits the DB three times for data that can be computed in one shot.\n\n```ruby\n# BAD: 3 queries\n@total_conversions  = current_user.conversions.count\n@recent_conversions = current_user.conversions.where('created_at > ?', 30.days.ago).count\n@older_conversions  = current_user.conversions.where('created_at BETWEEN ? AND ?', 60.days.ago, 30.days.ago).count\n\n# GOOD: 1 query, group in Ruby\ncutoffs = current_user.conversions.where('created_at > ?', 60.days.ago).pluck(:created_at)\n@total_conversions  = current_user.conversions.count  # still needed for all-time\n@recent_conversions = cutoffs.count { |t| t > 30.days.ago }\n@older_conversions  = cutoffs.count { |t| t <= 30.days.ago }\n```\n\n---\n\n### 4. Conversions#index — Unbounded Query (Memory Leak Risk)\n\n`conversions_controller.rb:5` loads **every conversion** for a user with no pagination:\n\n```ruby\n# BAD — loads ALL conversions for a user\n@conversions = current_user.conversions.order(created_at: :desc)\n```\n\nA power user with 500+ conversions loads them all into memory each visit.\n\n```ruby\n# GOOD — paginate (Kaminari already installed, used in admin/users_controller)\n@conversions = current_user.conversions.order(created_at: :desc).page(params[:page]).per(20)\n```\n\n---\n\n### 5. Cloudinary Download — Synchronous API Round-trip on Every Download\n\n`conversions_controller.rb:163–183` calls `Cloudinary::Api.resource(key, resource_type:)` — a live HTTP call to Cloudinary — on every download to detect resource type. This adds 100–500ms of latency to every single download and tries twice if the first type misses.\n\nThe resource type is known at upload time and should be persisted:\n\n```ruby\n# When creating the conversion output blob, store resource_type in blob metadata:\nblob.update!(metadata: blob.metadata.merge(\"cloudinary_resource_type\" => \"raw\"))\n\n# Then in download:\nresource_type = blob.metadata[\"cloudinary_resource_type\"] || \"raw\"\nurl = Cloudinary::Utils.cloudinary_url(key, resource_type: resource_type, ...)\nredirect_to url, allow_other_host: true\n# ponytail: eliminates 1-2 network hops per download\n```\n\n---\n\n### 6. ToolRegistry — O(n) Linear Scans\n\n`tool_registry.rb:289` (`find`) and `conversions_controller.rb:39` both do `Array#find` over all 26 tools. Simple to make O(1):\n\n```ruby\n# tool_registry.rb\nTOOLS_BY_ID         = tools.index_by { |t| t[:id] }.freeze\nTOOLS_BY_CLASS_NAME = tools.index_by { |t| t[:class_name] }.freeze\n\ndef self.find(tool_id) = TOOLS_BY_ID[tool_id]\ndef self.find_by_class(class_name) = TOOLS_BY_CLASS_NAME[class_name]\n```\n\nThen in `conversions_controller.rb:39`:\n```ruby\n@tool_meta = ToolRegistry.find_by_class(@conversion.tool_name)\n```\n\n---\n\n### 7. CleanupConversionsJob — Redundant COUNT Query\n\n`cleanup_conversions_job.rb:27` calls `conversions.count` and then iterates with `find_each`. The count is a wasted query since we can track deletions during iteration:\n\n```ruby\ndef purge_conversions(conversions)\n  count = 0\n  conversions.find_each { |c| c.destroy && count += 1 }\n  count\nend\n```\n\n---\n\n### Priority Order\n\n| # | Issue | Impact | Effort |\n|---|-------|--------|--------|\n| 1 | Missing DB indexes | High — full table scans | Low — one migration |\n| 2 | Admin dashboard 15 queries | High — admin UX + DB load | Low — wrap in `cache.fetch` |\n| 3 | Unbounded `conversions#index` | Medium — memory growth | Low — add `.page().per()` |\n| 4 | Cloudinary API round-trip on download | Medium — download latency | Medium — persist resource_type |\n| 5 | Analytics 3→1 query | Low — small table now | Low |\n| 6 | ToolRegistry O(n) | Negligible now | Low |\n| 7 | Cleanup count query | Negligible | Trivial |\n\nItems 1, 2, and 3 are the ones to ship first — they fix real query volume against a growing table. Items 4–7 are clean-ups that pay off under load.\n",
